      "urpx:hasCoverageClaim": {
        "@id": "sce:cc-tou-d-5-8pm-2025-11-15",
        "@type": "urpx:CoverageClaim",
        "urpx:completenessResult": {
          "@id": "urpx:partial"
        },
        "urpx:selfContained": true,
        "urpx:explainedBy": {
          "@type": "urpx:Evaluation",
          "@id": "sce:cc-tou-d-5-8pm-2025-11-15-evaluation",
          "urpx:evaluationKind": {
            "@id": "urpx:coverageEvaluation"
          },
          "urpx:description": "Two provisions on the Option 5-8 PM rate sheets are not represented in this filing. The first concerns the Baseline Credit of $(0.10449) per kWh: the rate sheet footnote states that it applies up to 100 percent of the Baseline Allocation, with additional allocations for customers who have a Heat Pump Water Heater, the allocations being set out in Preliminary Statement Part H. This filing carries the credit as an unbounded per-kilowatt-hour amount with no allocation quantity attached, so a consumer computing a bill from it will credit every kilowatt-hour rather than only those within the customer's baseline, overstating the credit for anyone above baseline. The second is the Option 5-8 PM-CPP variant, whose three prices sit on the same sheet this filing cites: a CPP Event Energy Charge of $0.80000 per kWh, a Summer on-peak non-event energy credit of $(0.15170) per kWh, and a Summer Season Maximum Available Credit of $(0.55677) per kWh for customers grandfathered into dual demand-response participation. None of the three is modeled, so this filing describes the non-CPP option only. Two further rate-sheet lines are out of scope rather than missing: the Deed Restricted Base Services Charge discount of $(0.334) per meter per day and the CARE and FERA discount percentages shown at 100.00 are cross-references to separate schedules, each filed as its own modifier overlay in this corpus.",
          "urpx:evaluatedOn": "2026-09-21T00:00:00"
        }
      }
    }
  }
